\begin{frame}{Racializing discourse}
  
  {\scriptsize
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Joshua: Est-ce que les Créoles parlent différemment des Cadiens? ... (45:03)
    \item Cynthia (Cajun): I went to the store.
          Mo'tais couru au store.
    \item Lucille (Cajun): Et toi t'as été plus élevée autour du créole.
          À la Ville Platte, les Noirs à la Ville Platte parlaient français cadien d'après ce que moi je me rappelle.
    \item Cynthia: Oui, il y a des Noirs qui parlent français comme nous-autres, mais il y a des Blancs qui parlent créole comme les Noirs.
  \end{enumerate}
  \hrule
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item \longtrans{Joshua: Do Creoles speak different from Cajuns? ...}
    \item \longtrans{Cynthia (Cajun): I went to the store.
          I went to the store.}
    \item \longtrans{Lucille (Cajun): And you were more raised around Creole.
          In Ville Platte, Black people in Ville Platte spoke Cajun French from what I remember.}
    \item \longtrans{Cynthia: Yes, there are Black people who speak French like us, but there are White people who speak Creole like Black people.}
  \end{enumerate}
  }
  RQ3: Is the discourse of Creoles and Cajuns in Louisiana indicative of the racialization of these groups in that it creates, polices, and maintains inequal boundaries?
\end{frame}